For the last year I've been out on my own, but before that I've spent nine months.

18
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:50,560
Prior to that, I spent nine and a half years working at Google.

19
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:54,960
Six of those years, I led the app ads sales team.

20
00:01:55,600 --> 00:02:01,200
So my team worked directly, mostly with game developers or the marketing teams within those games.

21
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:09,600
So I have a lot more inside knowledge as to like the product and how things actually work, so my perspective tends to be a little bit more unique.

22
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:12,720
It's really cool that you were in Google for so long.

23
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:27,840
And then, you know, kind of a little bit of the backstory that you've told me and shared publicly is that it was frustrating at times to work with customers where you kind of knew certain things that they could maybe be helpful but weren't really appropripriate for you to share as someone working at Google.

24
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:35,840
And so you did start a consulting company where you can say those things and maybe even be more directly helpful to the customers.

25
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:36,720
Yeah.

26
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:55,360
I think one of the things that this happens at any large company, right, where you feel that there's a lot of processes that you have to go through in order to actually be able to say anything, or there has to be so much data that's collected in order for Google to say, okay, this is how we can position this product.

27
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:04,240
And I think you guys understand better than anyone, people want to test things too.

28
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:19,680
So for us to have to wait years in some cases to be able to communicate to people what we feel like maybe we already know, but product or legal hasn't given us the stamp of approval to do so, I found to always be like really infuriating.

29
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:30,400
So now I I kind of offer a lot of those hot takes that I wasn't allowed to do before, but always with a grain of salt, like, you know, it's testing at the end of the day.

30
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:34,640
But yes, I do have I've seen thousands and thousands of these accounts.

31
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:38,240
And so I probably have a little bit more credibilibility when I make these statements.

32
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:42,240
Yeah, I was just talking to Shireen, it was actually the very last episode.

33
00:03:42,480 --> 00:03:45,200
So for those of you who didn't listen, go back and listen to that one.

34
00:03:45,760 --> 00:04:00,560
One of the things we talked about quite a bit in that episode was how important it is to take risks and how important it is to foster a culture of taking risks when you're doing user acquisition and trying ads because you have to take some risks.

35
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:01,840
You have to try some new things.

36
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:08,160
You have to kind of go out on a limb in order to find those things that are going to double your performance.

37
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:15,760
And that's one of the things she talked about was like they were able to double their performance over the course of a few years through like hundreds of tests.

38
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:17,840
And so it's kind of cool to hear that.

39
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,920
It's like when you're at Google, you can't, it's hard to tell people to take a risk that may lose them tens of thousands of dollars.

40
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:26,240
Like that's not what Google wants to do.

41
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:29,440
They want to only share the tried and true strategies.

42
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
And so, yeah, it's interesting to hear that one of the bigger frustrations was that you couldn't help people take those kinds of risks.

43
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:40,480
But it's those kinds of risks that get you that, like, you really optimize the performance over time.

44
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:52,640
I think one of the things that I felt most passionate about, and it's really how Candid Consulting came to be, was on the IOS front where Google has a very complicated relationship with Apple.

45
00:04:53,600 --> 00:05:06,080
And so we were so limited on what we were able to say when it was in regards to SK Ad Network and how Google's model was working and what we were rolling out.

46
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:12,720
And I would always advocate internally when I was there to say, like, hey, people just need some bit of information.

47
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:20,240
I know that we can't say definitely, this is what we're doing, or we may have to pivot later based on what Apple changes.

48
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:22,240
And people, I think, understand that.

49
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:24,320
What people don't like is silence.

50
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:37,120
And that's what I was really frustrated by a lot of times was just the silence because there was all of this back end complications that honestly I didn't always have visibility into.

51
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:42,240
They were happening, I think, a lot of times with like the engineers and the product teams and people very high up.

52
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:45,120
But yeah, it was like people just want to know something.

53
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,880
Like, can't we just tell them something?

54
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:50,640
Because like I can tell them little things, but it was like, no, no, no, not yet.

55
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:55,040
And so leaving has obviously enabled me to be able to share those insights.

56
00:05:55,520 --> 00:06:20,800
Let's just kick things off from the highest level overview then of like what is Google app campaigns and why should this audience of subscription app growth practitioners generally and I mean we have indie developers and CEOs and kind of a broad audience of folks who are going to be listening to this but like why should people be thinking about care about or what should they know about Google app campaigns?

57
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:29,040
If you have an app and you're starting to think about how you actually pay to get users to download that app, you're probably going to start looking at Google.

58
00:06:29,440 --> 00:06:35,520
Although I find a lot of people start with Meta first or if they're heavy on IOS, they'll start with Apple Search Ads.

59
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:40,400
But typically Google is the one first or second when they're looking to explore.

60
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:53,200
So Google app campaigns, if we take the history of it, I want to say it was probably eight years ago now, maybe nine, where Google actually, this was their first machine learning ads product.

61
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:59,120
And it was very novel because people, well, people didn't like the idea that Google was taking all this control.

62
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:02,880
They were basically saying, tell us how much you want to spend on a daily basis.

63
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:07,440
Tell us how much you're willing to pay for whatever it is, an install.

64
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:17,760
I think at the time it was mostly just installs, but now it can be how much you're willing to pay for a subscriber., someone to start trial and throw in some creative and we got it from here.

65
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,520
You don't need to tell us anything else.

66
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,640
And, and that's what's interesting is that that's the direction Google has moved with a lot of its products.

67
00:07:25,200 --> 00:07:31,840
And I'm not as familiar on the website of things, but people talk about Pmax and I think you're starting to see an expansion of this.

68
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:34,000
And the reason why is like it works.

69
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:35,360
It really does.

70
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:40,400
Like at the at the time, Google's app, ads, product contributed nothing.

71
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:47,200
And by and large in comparison to search, it's still a very small percentage of their ads revenue, but it has grown massively.

72
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:49,520
And so the product does really works.

73
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:53,360
So it started as UAC, Universal App Campaigns.

74
00:07:53,680 --> 00:07:55,920
That's what most people call it because it's easy.

75
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,200
Everyone loves an acronym.

76
00:07:57,280 --> 00:07:58,800
They dropped the U a couple of years ago.

77
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:00,240
So it's just app campaigns.

78
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:09,680
Basically, it allows you to serve across all different inventory types, but you don't have control over where, well, you kind of don't have control over where your ads serve.

79
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:14,000
So you can get into some of the fun little hacks to get a little more control.

80
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:24,240
That was one of the frustrations when UAC came out was that it limited so much control, but that tradeoff was the performance, you know, and we see Meta going that direction as well, giving you less specific control.

81
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:34,400
But then the algorithms have just gotten so good, both on Google Meta and other ad products, where the algorithm is going to end up making better decisions, and that's why they did it.

82
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:42,960
So what would you say are some of the key benefits then of Google app campaigns, and then we can get to some of the drawbacks that we've already kind of discussed some of them.

83
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:48,320
Whenever I talk about Google, you really have to talk about it for Android and for IOS.

84
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:56,400
I feel like with Meta, people, they don't really care too much, or people don't, I don't notice people, you know, sort of like separating the two and talking about them separately.

85
00:08:56,720 --> 00:09:07,600
But I feel with app campaigns, or I'll just say UAC, it is really good critical because on Android you have the benefit of actually serving your ads in the Play Store.

86
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:09,920
That's like super high intention.

87
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:12,400
You can't get that inventory anywhere else.

88
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:12,800
Right.

89
00:09:13,120 --> 00:09:14,400
So that just doesn't exist.

90
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:15,600
You can't buy it.

91
00:09:15,760 --> 00:09:17,280
It's not like part of another network.

92
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:23,120
So I think that's really the main advantage of UAC when it comes to Android.

93
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:25,840
And also there's a lot of other properties, right?

94
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,040
You have Google Search and YouTube.

95
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:30,240
So there's a lot more that you can leverage there too.

96
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:33,280
But by and large, the Play Store is huge on iOS.

97
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:33,840
Yeah.

98
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:36,000
Like Google's actually not as competitive.

99
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:39,040
But I really don't feel it can be overlooked.

100
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:43,680
You still have Google Search, which again, you can't buy search from anywhere else.

101
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:47,840
And you have YouTube, which still is like the number one app.

102
00:09:48,400 --> 00:09:52,960
It's like the most downloaded app in the world, I think, not just in the US.

103
00:09:53,840 --> 00:10:01,280
So those two pockets of inventory are really, you know, those are huge for you to be able to access and be able to advertise your app.

104
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:21,120
And, and, you know, this is one of the things about Meta and why a lot of apps are finding success right now is that between the Facebook app and the Instagram app, you're just getting such broad exposure, but then Google takes it even like multiple steps further, like YouTube inside the Play Store across search across their display ad networks.

105
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:26,080
It's just a massive scale that is hard to access in any other way.

106
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:31,600
What would you say then are some of the drawbacks that come to mind with UAC?

107
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:50,080
Going back to the controls, it can be really challenging if you're testing in particular, if you're like, oh, I really want to see if how these videos perform on Android, it is really challenging to try and get your videos to get any meaningful traffic.

108
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:51,440
People can do it, right?

109
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:54,080
And it depends on the vertical of your app.

110
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:57,040
But again, you have the Play Store.

111
00:10:57,280 --> 00:10:59,200
It's going to be really high converting traffic.

112
00:10:59,440 --> 00:11:03,920
I always liken the Play Store traffic to branded search.

113
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:08,240
Like when we talk about, you know, when you used to have your, you know, branded keywords, right?

114
00:11:08,400 --> 00:11:14,720
It's like you're reaching people at the highest intent moment possible when they're actually searching for something they want something.

115
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:16,640
And that's kind of what the Play Store is to.

116
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:18,800
They're grabbing them right at that last minute.

117
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:32,720
You know, being able to really diversify where your ads are showing, that can be a big help challenge or the opposite of trying to force and get traffic somewhere else, getting it away.

118
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:37,280
Like I was talking with a client yesterday, all of their traffic's flowing to display.

119
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:40,800
And we're like, how can we force it over here?

120
00:11:41,440 --> 00:11:49,680
And, you know, besides really paying a lot more, like manipulating your bids, which isn't always feasible, that can be a big drawback.

121
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:54,320
And so I think a lot of people despise Google's products for that reason.

122
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:57,280
But as we were kind of pointing out, it's like it's all tradeoffs.

123
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:00,880
So you trade off some of that control for reach, for the high intent, things like that.

124
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:12,000
And we've kind of been hitting on this already, but any other contrasts you want to paint between UAC and the other major kind of user acquisition channels, TikTok, Meta, Apple search ads?

125
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:13,440
Oh yeah.

126
00:12:14,080 --> 00:12:18,960
I think one thing that people look at is a lot of people are like, oh, it's just a it's an app network, right?

127
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:21,440
We're using that product to distribute our app.

128
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,120
But you have to think about it.

129
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:25,920
If you have, let's just use Meta as the primary example.

130
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:31,280
They have their two main sources of traffic are Facebook and Instagram, right?

131
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:35,600
And most of that is app specific.

132
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:38,960
I mean, if you're doing an app campaign, it's just going to serve there.

133
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:47,040
On Google, we're talking about, let's talk about like the five primary sources of traffic that you get with your Android campaign.

134
00:12:47,280 --> 00:12:48,160
You have the Play Store.

135
00:12:48,880 --> 00:12:52,800
You have Google Search, which is mostly done in a browser.

136
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:54,800
It's not like in an app, okay?

137
00:12:55,120 --> 00:12:56,160
So it's in a browser.

138
00:12:57,040 --> 00:13:03,920
You have YouTube, which is probably pretty heavy on apps, but there actually is still quite a bit of it that happens in a browser.

139
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:11,040
You have AdMob traffic, which is ads in other apps that's part of Google's network.

140
00:13:11,280 --> 00:13:20,400
And then you have what they call MGDN, which is mobile Google Display Network, which is ads in browsers on websites, mobile websites.

141
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:33,360
So a lot more of Google's mobile traffic is served in browser in comparison to these other like app native platforms like a meta or a TikTok, right?

142
00:13:33,520 --> 00:13:34,880
This just like doesn't exist on the web.

143
00:13:35,440 --> 00:13:36,960
And so you have to really consider that.

144
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:41,280
And that's the piece that you really have to understand, especially when it comes to iOS.

145
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:44,640
That's why Google, I think, had so many challenges for a long time.

146
00:13:45,520 --> 00:13:49,440
SK Ad Network wasn't even counting web to app conversions.

147
00:13:50,080 --> 00:14:02,880
This is like, you know, we had cases of some clients where 99% of their traffic was coming from search and scan wasn't capturing any of those conversions, like zero of them.

148
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:14,160
Now with the scan four Auto that has changed, but there's still a lot of limitations and I don't think people realize how unique Google's platform is compared to some of the other big players.

149
00:14:14,560 --> 00:14:24,560
And one of the things you had shared previously, we did a webinar, which by the way, this the webinar might be a good add-on to this if you want to go back and listen to Ashley's webinar, just slides and all that.

150
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:27,280
So kind of a nice complement to this conversation.

151
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:29,440
But you also mentioned Play Browse.

152
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:38,480
So, so, in within the Play Store, you can actually buy ads where if people are browsing around looking for apps, they're going to see those ads.

153
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:42,400
Is that serve much traffic and and is that performing well?

154
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:43,680
Nobody knows.

155
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:45,280
David, that's the thing.

156
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:47,120
That's the thing.

157
00:14:47,440 --> 00:14:56,240
That's the other reason why people don't like this is because you have really limited visibility into where your ads are showing.

158
00:14:56,720 --> 00:15:09,680
So in the Google Ads, like in the front end, if you're in looking and you're managing your campaigns, you'll be able to see a break out of the four different inventory sources.

159
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:17,600
So you can see they lump it as search traffic, search network, search partners, which usually is like a really small percentage.

160
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:19,920
If it's high, that should be a warning sign to you.

161
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:25,040
And then there's this other one called Google Display Network, and that's what we'll talk about in just a second.

162
00:15:25,520 --> 00:15:26,480
And then there's YouTube.

163
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:31,680
But again, what people don't realize is like what's included in those?

164
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:38,720
So if we're talking about search, yes, it searches on goches in play.

165
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:47,120
While you don't have a way of seeing the breakout, you have a really good, I was telling people like, if you have a really good Google rep, you can.

166
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:48,880
They do have the ability to see it.

167
00:15:48,960 --> 00:15:51,520
They have to have the right access, but they can show you the breakout.

168
00:15:51,920 --> 00:15:56,480
So there's google dot com searches, there's play search, and that's all included in search.

169
00:15:56,960 --> 00:16:11,360
And then when we talk about the display network, that is the admob traffic, a lot of times it's the mgbn, which I was talking about, it's like website images or ads, and then it also can include play browses.

170
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:18,480
That's the tricky part is like you're trying to figure out, oh, okay, well, is this not working because of this or is this happening?

171
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:21,280
You're making a lot of assumptions about things.

172
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:26,240
And so a lot of times it can be hard because you're a little bit blind to what's actually at play.

173
00:16:26,880 --> 00:16:44,560
But overall, the combination of all these things and Google's algorithm that that tries to figure out where things are going to be performance, it does seem like overall it is a high quality source of and especially on Android, potentially very high intent traffic as well.

174
00:16:45,280 --> 00:16:45,680
Absolutely.

175
00:16:48,320 --> 00:16:50,720
on the algorithm to sort through.

176
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:57,040
There are really high quality sources of traffic that are flowing through UAC.

177
00:16:57,680 --> 00:16:58,080
Oh, yeah.

178
00:16:58,320 --> 00:17:00,720
I don't think that's undeniable.

179
00:17:01,200 --> 00:17:08,880
On Android, like we were talking about, and also on IOS, you have Google Search, which again, like, I just don't think people understand.

180
00:17:09,040 --> 00:17:11,120
It's like, you can't buy that anywhere else.

181
00:17:11,760 --> 00:17:19,280
You can't get those types of users who are actively querying for a product that you have anywhere else.

182
00:17:19,440 --> 00:17:21,200
And so that can't be ignored.

183
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:24,160
And like you mentioned, the sheer volume of people.

184
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:35,680
Like I know a lot of people, you can get good scale on a lot of these other platforms, but I do think a lot of those other platforms have demographics and audiences that they maybe skew a little bit more towards.

185
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:45,520
Where I feel like if you think about Google and all the products that we just listed, you're likely to find a little bit of everyone across all of these.

186
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:47,760
You know, everyone's looking at YouTube at some point.

187
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:49,520
Everyone uses Google Search.

188
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:53,840
If you have an Android device, you're sort of required to use the Play Store.

189
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:57,280
We don't have to get into those requirements, but you know what I'm saying.

190
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:04,800
I just feel like it's something that can't be ignored and there is a really good high quality user that exists out there.

