And then also the orientations.

388
00:35:36,960 --> 00:35:50,080
I mostly default to portrait, especially if we're talking about iOS, but on Android you can't overlook the landscape orientation because a lot of people still do watch their YouTube videos in landscape mode.

389
00:35:50,480 --> 00:35:52,000
And then what about image assets?

390
00:35:52,240 --> 00:35:56,160
Because those are the three primary assets that you provide to Google, right?

391
00:35:56,480 --> 00:36:06,560
So within each ad group, you're posting text assets, image assets and video assets and the algorithm is just figuring it out.

392
00:36:07,040 --> 00:36:10,720
Yes, usually combining some, you know, aspects of that.

393
00:36:11,040 --> 00:36:15,200
Usually text is always with some component with image or with video.

394
00:36:16,080 --> 00:36:18,000
But yeah, images are used a lot.

395
00:36:18,320 --> 00:36:22,240
Also, images are used sometimes in the Play store too.

396
00:36:22,640 --> 00:36:25,120
So again on Android, you'll notice the theme here.

397
00:36:25,200 --> 00:36:32,720
I talk a lot about the differences between the two, but on Android, I over index for landscape orientation images.

398
00:36:33,040 --> 00:36:36,880
I, as of recent, I've kind of taken the dance that I'm aiming for like an API.

399
00:36:36,880 --> 00:36:40,880
Aiming for like an eighty twenty rule for images, eighty percent of them on Android.

400
00:36:40,960 --> 00:36:48,320
I want to be in landscape and then I'll use the remaining twenty percent for like square or portrait just because landscape always gets more traffic.

401
00:36:48,880 --> 00:36:56,320
And Google also has the option where you can like they have the AI generator assets now where you can actually type in text and create your own.

402
00:36:56,640 --> 00:37:06,320
One thing I tell people is like you don't have to have a fully designed image asset because Google overlays so much within your asset.

403
00:37:06,480 --> 00:37:08,000
So they're going to pull in your app icon.

404
00:37:08,240 --> 00:37:11,280
They will in most cases pull in a install button.

405
00:37:11,520 --> 00:37:14,080
It says like close or install a lot of times.

406
00:37:14,960 --> 00:37:16,960
And then they even like pull in a rating.

407
00:37:17,520 --> 00:37:23,520
A lot of times people don't realize that your app store rating is pulled in to the actual asset too.

408
00:37:24,320 --> 00:37:35,840
So if you have a fully designed image asset with like a bunch of text and an image and a call to action and your rating, if you're calling that out for whatever reason, it's really redundant.

409
00:37:36,240 --> 00:37:37,360
Google is already doing it.

410
00:37:38,080 --> 00:37:40,560
So I usually tell people like use a stock image.

411
00:37:41,440 --> 00:37:43,040
They work just as well a lot of times.

412
00:37:43,360 --> 00:37:43,840
They're free.

413
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:47,520
You can just search for them right as you're creating your ad groups.

414
00:37:48,320 --> 00:37:51,760
And grab, grab a few of those and see what works.

415
00:37:51,920 --> 00:37:53,760
You don't have to, I mean, you can test it.

416
00:37:53,840 --> 00:37:58,560
You can, and by test, I mean putting something else in there and see if it gets traffic.

417
00:37:59,200 --> 00:38:01,920
You can't run like an AB test on an image asset.

418
00:38:02,080 --> 00:38:04,960
So you got to just kind of like, you know, throw it up there and see if it sticks.

419
00:38:05,280 --> 00:38:14,400
So then the recommendation here then is like you don't like hire a graphic designer and have this polish like what you would typically perceive as an image ad.

420
00:38:15,360 --> 00:38:21,040
Like you said, with like a clear call to action, a subtext highlighting your favorite feature, whatever.

421
00:38:21,200 --> 00:38:33,200
It's like the assets should the image assets should just be very kind of plain, no text at all or minimal text or like again, it's not like a hard and fast rule.

422
00:38:33,760 --> 00:38:45,680
I haven't seen those, the ones that are fully designed, at least for the accounts that I work on and a lot of my clients, I haven't seen them get as much volume as the ones that are just images.

423
00:38:46,480 --> 00:38:49,920
And again, the whole concept is because it's already overlaid anyway.

424
00:38:50,480 --> 00:39:05,200
So in a lot of cases, if you're finding that those are not working for you or they're not, you know, like the click through rates really low, then sure go ahead and design a fleet, but I don't think you necessarily need that in order to get started.

425
00:39:05,840 --> 00:39:12,560
How do you look at the performance or can you even look at the performance of individual assets in UAC?

426
00:39:13,040 --> 00:39:16,320
Yes, you can, but I think there's a difference.

427
00:39:16,480 --> 00:39:26,720
A lot of when I was working at Google and working with a lot of games, they would get really frustrated that they couldn't see what the combination of assets produced.

428
00:39:27,120 --> 00:39:37,120
So like they wanted to be able to see, okay, well, did this specific text plus this image perform better than this text with the same image.

429
00:39:37,760 --> 00:39:39,360
Google won't let you do that.

430
00:39:39,760 --> 00:39:49,040
They're not showing you that level of granularity, but you absolutely can see, like, hey, is this text asset performing better than another text asset?

431
00:39:49,760 --> 00:39:51,600
And so you can break it down quite a bit.

432
00:39:51,920 --> 00:39:54,080
There's actually a performance rating in Google.

433
00:39:54,480 --> 00:39:59,840
If you pull up your account, it will tell you, Google has a performance rating, how your asset is.

434
00:40:00,080 --> 00:40:03,600
Was it like best, good, low, and then like learning?

435
00:40:03,920 --> 00:40:05,600
Just don't pay attention to it.

436
00:40:07,520 --> 00:40:08,400
I was joking or something.

437
00:40:08,640 --> 00:40:11,680
I'm like, it's five columns that you can't remove.

438
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:16,240
They serve almost no purpose, at least when I'm optimizing an account.

439
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:24,480
And the reason why is that performance rating is based on the scale of the campaign and the click through rate, so the engagement.

440
00:40:24,640 --> 00:40:28,240
So it's kind of like how much can Google serve this asset?

441
00:40:28,480 --> 00:40:32,400
And then that's what the rating is based on, not necessarily how it's performing.

442
00:40:33,120 --> 00:40:33,840
Performing, right?

443
00:40:34,080 --> 00:40:36,880
Like your actual metric that you want to optimize towards.

444
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:41,200
So if you're new and that's how you're approaching it, just go ahead and ignore that.

445
00:40:41,920 --> 00:40:46,000
Focus on whatever you're optimizing for your CPI or your CPA.

446
00:40:46,320 --> 00:40:47,840
How do you actually look at the performance of it?

447
00:40:48,000 --> 00:40:50,240
So yeah, you know, you do the asset reporting.

448
00:40:50,560 --> 00:40:54,880
I pay a lot of attention to where traffic is serving.

449
00:40:55,120 --> 00:40:58,240
So I've talked a lot about like all the different inventory types.

450
00:40:58,880 --> 00:41:08,480
So a lot of times if I'm like, well, why is this ad group performing, you know, worse than this one or vice versa, a lot of times I'll pull in the field.

451
00:41:08,800 --> 00:41:14,960
There's a way to segment it so I can actually see the percentage of traffic that's flowing to search versus display or YouTube.

452
00:41:15,760 --> 00:41:19,840
And that, to me, is a really helpful tool.

453
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:22,880
It guides me to understand like what might be happening here.

454
00:41:23,360 --> 00:41:24,720
I also, well, Google doesn't.

455
00:41:25,440 --> 00:41:30,000
like you might have to create it as a custom column, I actually spend a lot of time looking at CPMs.

456
00:41:30,320 --> 00:41:35,600
I don't know if other people do that quite as much, but I notice some big differences.

457
00:41:35,840 --> 00:41:42,000
Again, like you have to do some guessing work on what's happening here because not all of it is exposed.

458
00:41:42,480 --> 00:41:49,840
So a lot of times if I notice like a big decline in performance, then I check out, okay, well, was there also a decline in CPM?

459
00:41:50,240 --> 00:41:55,200
A lot of times cheaper CPMs mean worse traffic, not always, but a lot of times.

460
00:41:55,840 --> 00:42:00,880
So that's another thing that I pay attention to when I'm going through and actually like optimizing for performance.

461
00:42:01,680 --> 00:42:08,320
Are there any gotches to all this as far as in any way to point the algorithm in specific directions?

462
00:42:08,640 --> 00:42:19,120
You know, I've heard folks and of course you would never say this, but I've heard folks say that Google is going to optimize toward the things that make them the most money.

463
00:42:19,520 --> 00:42:35,360
And so kind of to your point about like looking at CPMs, if you're getting like lower CPMs, it may be that it's being served to lower quality traffic and they're hitting your budget and you're still getting the performance you want, but maybe you could be getting better performance.

464
00:42:35,920 --> 00:42:36,720
How do you think about that?

465
00:42:37,600 --> 00:42:39,120
Yeah, you have to pay attention to it.

466
00:42:39,520 --> 00:42:47,840
One of the things that people, again, don't like when Google rolled out UAC is that it blended a lot of the traffic.

467
00:42:48,240 --> 00:42:55,760
And so all of a sudden you have your highest, like they can serve at the highest performing traffic, they know that that's going to convert.

468
00:42:56,400 --> 00:42:59,680
And then they serve the crappy stuff and it all averages out.

469
00:43:00,000 --> 00:43:01,760
And people are like, well, I don't want the crap.

470
00:43:02,080 --> 00:43:04,080
You know, I just want the premium stuff.

471
00:43:04,320 --> 00:43:06,240
And so you do have to be on top of it.

472
00:43:06,560 --> 00:43:10,880
Those are the, like I was mentioning, like the CPMs where your traffic is serving.

473
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:12,560
You can to some degree see.

474
00:43:12,960 --> 00:43:18,880
see what sites or apps your ads are serving in or what YouTube videos they're serving in.

475
00:43:19,120 --> 00:43:20,800
So I pay attention to that sometimes.

476
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:25,280
Like if it's a significant volume and I'm like, well, this is just a crap site.

477
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:26,560
Like it's super spammy.

478
00:43:26,720 --> 00:43:28,640
There's like, no, forty ads on there.

479
00:43:29,440 --> 00:43:40,640
I don't want my clients to be included in that type of inventory, but And so you can actually go in there and say, like, don't show on this site specifically or you would even just say, don't show in this type of inventory.

480
00:43:41,120 --> 00:43:47,600
No, not on this type of inventory, although I think you can do it for certain apps, but it does block it at an account level.

481
00:43:48,640 --> 00:43:52,400
Not just at a campaign level, but that doesn't usually cause too much issue.

482
00:43:52,800 --> 00:43:54,320
But yeah, so that's a reality.

483
00:43:54,480 --> 00:43:57,360
You do have to kind of make sure that you're staying on top of it.

484
00:43:57,680 --> 00:44:01,760
I know a lot of people are like, oh, you just like, you know, set it and forget it and it keeps doing it.

485
00:44:01,920 --> 00:44:15,200
It's like, yes, if you're okay with that, if you're okay knowing that you're getting some of the crap and you can't avoid the crap altogether, but you can try and minimize it or try and increase the, the high quality stuff.

486
00:44:15,600 --> 00:44:30,320
And would you also then be looking at certain, like, images or text that's hitting those lower CPMs that are kind of giving you a hint that maybe at the Is that how you would kind of approach that?

487
00:44:30,800 --> 00:44:33,200
I did it yesterday for one of my clients.

488
00:44:33,920 --> 00:44:44,400
This one's a little tricky that I find is like in this particular case they were getting a lot of traffic from certain videos and that was fueling the majority of their scale.

489
00:44:44,720 --> 00:44:47,120
Like that's where all of the volume was coming from.

490
00:44:47,680 --> 00:44:52,480
So initially I was really resistant to just being like, eh, can I get rid of it?

491
00:44:52,720 --> 00:44:56,000
Because then what can happen is that that will tank the whole campaign.

492
00:44:56,480 --> 00:44:58,880
All of a sudden it's like we don't have any conversions.

493
00:45:00,560 --> 00:45:02,080
It's just like we're just going to die out.

494
00:45:02,800 --> 00:45:05,040
So you have to sometimes be delicate with that.

495
00:45:05,280 --> 00:45:09,760
It's like a balance between how much you're removing at once.

496
00:45:10,480 --> 00:45:13,600
And so in that case, I kind of went in cautiously.

497
00:45:13,840 --> 00:45:15,600
I was like, okay, I see these videos.

498
00:45:16,160 --> 00:45:18,400
Yeah, they're they have a super high CPA.

499
00:45:18,640 --> 00:45:22,400
Sometimes it's also important to like look at your even further down funnel metrics.

500
00:45:22,560 --> 00:45:24,000
Like maybe the CPA is okay.

501
00:45:24,240 --> 00:45:25,040
It's within target.

502
00:45:25,760 --> 00:45:28,880
But in this particular case, the ROAS was crap.

503
00:45:29,120 --> 00:45:30,080
Then I had to make the call.

504
00:45:30,320 --> 00:45:43,280
And so I just slowly started kind of removing those assets so that it didn't cause the campaign to just have no conversions and then give it a week and then after that I have to pull them all because Google's trying to get away with something.

505
00:45:44,800 --> 00:45:45,200
Got you.

506
00:45:45,840 --> 00:45:57,440
So we talked about structuring campaigns early on and kind of that first, like just launch with one app campaign and then you know, do your testing in four or five ad groups.

507
00:45:58,000 --> 00:46:01,760
How do you scale that up or like what are your kind of campaign structuring and testing?

508
00:46:02,160 --> 00:46:03,600
When do you layer on more campaigns?

509
00:46:03,760 --> 00:46:04,800
How do you do that smartly?

510
00:46:05,280 --> 00:46:12,800
Well, layering on more campaigns, I think in any specific geo, maybe you don't want to do it, although it can be a hack.

511
00:46:13,280 --> 00:46:13,520
sometimes.

512
00:46:13,920 --> 00:46:16,560
I've noticed some people where they're like, I'm not getting enough scale.

513
00:46:16,640 --> 00:46:28,160
Like, even though, you know, I've seen people with budgets of like, you know, $100,000 a day and just because they want to signal to the system, like, spend my money and it won't sometimes.

514
00:46:28,480 --> 00:46:34,800
So, one hack, this is definitely not a Google best practice, but it's something I think I've learned more.

515
00:46:34,960 --> 00:46:47,360
So, like, as a practitioner doing this all the time, sometimes just like duplicating the campaigns, for whatever reason, like you duplicate it and that signal to Google is like, oh, we have something new to go out and we have to go test it.

516
00:46:47,360 --> 00:46:48,640
And so then you might start to see more.

517
00:46:49,120 --> 00:46:53,360
scale, whereas that existing campaign had just kind of remained very steady.

518
00:46:54,240 --> 00:46:56,160
So that's like one hacky way to do it.

519
00:46:56,320 --> 00:47:00,560
But again, like the scaling piece is like it's just kind of slow and steady.

520
00:47:00,960 --> 00:47:10,640
I think it's hard if you're reaching your max capacity in one market to really force more scale without becoming really aggressive with your bids.

521
00:47:11,520 --> 00:47:14,320
So then I start to look at other markets that might perform well.

522
00:47:14,800 --> 00:47:16,000
And then what about ad groups?

523
00:47:16,320 --> 00:47:18,800
Are you constantly like adding or removing ad groups?

524
00:47:19,040 --> 00:47:24,480
As you said, like if you're targeting specific kind of messaging or talking about specific features.

525
00:47:25,040 --> 00:47:34,160
Are you or is that an area that you're actively testing, like add a new ad group more focused on this feature versus that feature and remove that ad group that wasn't performing?

526
00:47:35,040 --> 00:47:35,120
Yeah.

527
00:47:35,520 --> 00:47:44,400
So, um, a lot of times what you'll find though is that they want the new ones that you add in, like, for whatever reason, Google favors like their first child.

528
00:47:45,200 --> 00:47:54,640
So, like the first, the first ad group that you create, it I've just seen it happen so many times, that is the one that they're like, we're we're running with this one.

529
00:47:55,040 --> 00:47:58,880
Even if another one seems to be performing better, it doesn't always get as much scale.

530
00:47:59,360 --> 00:48:00,480
So, yes, I do.

531
00:48:01,040 --> 00:48:10,080
add in new ad groups, like, you know, around the holidays, for example, if we're testing a new holiday theme, I put it in a specific ad group that also makes it easier to manage, right?

532
00:48:10,320 --> 00:48:17,120
Like, I don't forget about it later on that, like, oh, we're running like Halloween creatives, like, that's labeled separately.

533
00:48:17,440 --> 00:48:19,680
It wasn't mixed in with a bunch of others.

534
00:48:19,920 --> 00:48:21,520
So, like, I can just pause that ad group.

535
00:48:22,160 --> 00:48:33,040
If there's something seasonal that's going on, like, you know, I work with some apps where natural disasters, for example, that happen, that's when they see peaks in their traffic.

536
00:48:33,280 --> 00:48:45,200
And so we run individual, like, natural disaster ad groups like if a hurricane's happening or a tornado or, you know, if it's avalanche season or whatever, we have those separate and test out those things.

537
00:48:45,840 --> 00:48:55,360
So yes, I am constantly going in and trying new ones, but a lot of times they won't get meaningful volume to really be able to say like, was it worth it?

538
00:48:55,440 --> 00:48:57,280
So you might have to leave them on for a little bit longer.

539
00:48:57,760 --> 00:49:15,520
And then with that kind of first child as you called it, the the kind of one ad group that the app campaign kind of preferences, would you then start to expand the assets in that ad group as a kind of an experimentation of adding some new themes and other stuff in that high performing ad group?

540
00:49:15,840 --> 00:49:15,920
Yeah.

541
00:49:16,240 --> 00:49:21,120
I mean, I think a lot of times I try and just what I've noticed, I don't hear people talk about it as often.

542
00:49:21,680 --> 00:49:27,760
So, prior to Google, I worked at Pandora and that was more like a brand's plays.

543
00:49:28,240 --> 00:49:33,680
And one thing we talked a lot more about there, keep in mind, this is really long time ago, but was ad fatigue.

544
00:49:34,400 --> 00:49:36,880
And I don't think that we talk about that quite as much.

545
00:49:37,120 --> 00:49:48,720
And I mean, maybe we do in the UA space, but I think that's something that people need to realize is that even in those high performing ad groups, right, at some point, people are just tired of seeing the same thing.

546
00:49:48,880 --> 00:49:49,520
thing over and over.

547
00:49:49,760 --> 00:49:53,360
It's just hit the same audience too many times and they've tuned it out.

548
00:49:53,520 --> 00:50:00,480
And so, or Google has just gotten into this, like, habit of, like, showing it to the same, like, on the same inventory or to the same people.

549
00:50:01,120 --> 00:50:15,440
So even making a small, tiny adjustment, actually, like if you had something that said, buy it today and you changed it to buy today, you took out the it, that sends Google into a whole new learning.

550
00:50:15,760 --> 00:50:17,600
It's like, oop, we have to figure it out.

551
00:50:17,760 --> 00:50:20,960
So it's not a new concept, but to Google, it is.

552
00:50:21,920 --> 00:50:23,840
And so it'll go out and test something new.

553
00:50:24,480 --> 00:50:27,040
And the ad isn't fatigued quite as much.

554
00:50:27,680 --> 00:50:46,960
So we've kind of weaved it into this entire conversation, but any other kind of troubleshooting or analysis tips on, like, if things are not performing well, like these are the first places to look, and like how do you troubleshoot campaigns and budget and things to get optimal performance?

555
00:50:47,440 --> 00:50:52,000
I mean, I guess that's why people bring me on, it's like that's what I spend a lot of my time doing.

556
00:50:52,320 --> 00:50:53,680
It's what my team does as well.

557
00:50:54,320 --> 00:50:58,240
The people that actually like know the platform pretty well, I don't think this comes as any surprise.

558
00:50:58,720 --> 00:51:02,240
Everyone uses that, the segment called like networks.

559
00:51:03,280 --> 00:51:10,560
And that's again where you can see at an asset level how your ads are serving across all those different inventory sources.

560
00:51:11,280 --> 00:51:12,880
That is so important.

561
00:51:13,760 --> 00:51:16,960
You really need to understand where those ads are serving.

562
00:51:17,600 --> 00:51:24,960
And, you know, I again, I did an audit for somebody just this week and they were like, well, we don't understand like why we're getting kind of this crap traffic.

563
00:51:25,120 --> 00:51:34,000
And I'm like, it's because all of these assets are serving on the display network and you're not getting any of that high quality search traffic.

564
00:51:34,800 --> 00:51:39,440
And so if you don't know to look look at something like that, then you're like, Oh, Google's just not working.

565
00:51:39,520 --> 00:51:41,280
So now we had to go back and figure out ways.

566
00:51:41,440 --> 00:51:47,200
How do you force it in some ways to spend in these other areas or test those other areas?

567
00:51:47,760 --> 00:51:53,760
I guess my big tip for people is like, don't just assume that it's doing what you think it should be doing.

568
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:55,520
Sometimes it'll go rogue on you.

569
00:51:56,080 --> 00:52:00,000
And so you do have to kind of know how to manage it and also what metrics matter.

570
00:52:00,160 --> 00:52:03,200
As I said, I pay a lot of attention to CPMs.

571
00:52:03,760 --> 00:52:06,400
I know a lot of other people pay attention to IPMs.

572
00:52:07,040 --> 00:52:12,160
I don't tend to pay attention to that because of the, like, we talked about it extensively.

573
00:52:14,240 --> 00:52:19,280
So your IPM on search is going to be really different than your IPM on display.

574
00:52:19,680 --> 00:52:23,440
So I think that's kind of unfair to hold them to the same bar.

575
00:52:24,000 --> 00:52:33,840
Anyway, so I tend to look at those other, like early metrics to determine if something ultimately is going to perform well or why maybe it is or isn't performing well.

576
00:52:34,400 --> 00:52:55,600
Sounds like it's a trust but verify situation where to use UAC, you have to trust the algorithm because it's like you just can't get around having to trust that it's going to get you performance, but then you verify that it's actually giving you performancece you want and then kind of subtly nudging it in different directions with all these techniques that we've been talking about.

577
00:52:55,920 --> 00:52:56,240
Absolutely.

578
00:52:56,480 --> 00:52:57,360
That might be my new tag.

579
00:52:58,320 --> 00:52:59,040
Trust but verify.

